= Summary
Today work has been astonishingly miserable and pathetic. I started off by
performing the planned maintenance on the proxy server, tweaking some of its
files, and then moving on to the `ctest` extension matters.

There I worked on the codata pattern detection algorithm. The only thing left
before starting to test it out was to implement the function that would remove
the codata off of the module where it had been found.

That I got to finish after, much like yesterday, struggling to clear up my mind.
Afterwards, though, I just stared at the screen. Quite literally, the only thing
left was to start the damn tests --- Nothing; just staring.

In thinking this was a waste of time because I wasn't even stuck on a problem, I
decided to go read about Common Lisp. I had recently heard it was a nice tool to
get things done fast while still getting performance without worrying about it.

Considering the desperate need I am of showcasing stuff I've done, this was
fairly appealing to me. Rust makes me worry about details I need not bother
about, and Lean makes me want to forget programming and just do proofs.

I started a few projects earlier this year. Some of those include a TSP solver
based on a triangulation-MST-DST heuristic, a Stanford GraphBase
reimplementation and a theory-building disassembler that uses MML.

None of those I've finished, and my recent interests on theorem proving have
made the idea of finishing them look even more dire. But I didn't know the
rabbit hole I was getting into when I looked into Common Lisp.

Of course, "lispers" use Emacs as their editor of choice, so I had to install
and (re)learn Emacs. There's other alternatives to get up and running quickly,
but I failed to set them up in my workstation.

That was the main distraction today. I have failed in delivering all problems
from the Lean functional programming book chapter on monads. And I have only
solved one more proof in the chapter before tactics of the theorem proving book.

Peak mediocrity, and a mind that doesn't want to do more even though I provide
it with plenty of healthy stimulus. What a worthless, miserable, useless piece
of uttter shit.

= Blockers
None.

= Plan for the week
Let us hope tomorrow I can finally stop staring at the god damned screen and
start testing the codata detection algorithm. Lean work will continue as per the
usual, though of course, misery seems to abound, so who knows.

It's very likely I will go back to handwritten notes and work, because a screen
seems to dull the mind even when a good challenge is posed at the screen starer.
